<SubSection title="A Brief History of Soft Robotics" id="History of Robotics">
<p>1920: The word "robot" is introduced by Karel Capek in a play.
1942: Isaac Asimov, a Prof of Biochemistry, introduced the Three Laws of Robotics</p>

<Image src="/soro/Unimate.jpg">
<span class="fw-bold">Three Laws of Robotics</span>: CAPTION.
</Image>

<p>1954: The first programmable robot is introduced.
1968: Unimate, an early industrial robot is commercialized.</p>

<Image src="/soro/Unimate.jpg">
<span class="fw-bold">Early Industrial Robot</span>: CAPTION.
</Image>
</SubSection>

<SubSection title="McKibbens Muscle" id="First soft actuator: McKibbens Muscle">
<p>In 1950, NAME published an article describing the McKibbens Muscle.</p>

<Image src="/soro/McKibbens_Schematic.jpg">
<span class="fw-bold">McKibbens Muscles</span>: A McKibbens muscle is composed of a internal elastomer balloon and an external braided sheath. The 'muscle' contracts or shortens when inflated with air.
</Image>

<p>The muscle was used in a proethetic hand designed for their own child.</p>

<Image src="/soro/Hands.jpg">
<span class="fw-bold">Application of McKibbens Muscles</span>: A McKibbens muscle is composed of a internal elastomer balloon and an external braided sheath. The 'muscle' contracts or shortens when inflated with air. In this case, the muscle is connected to tendons of the prosthetic fingers caused them to bend when the McKibbens mescle is actuated.
</Image>
</SubSection>

<SubSection title="Elastomer Pneumatic Actuators" id="Elastomer Pneumatic Actuators">
<p>In 1990, Dr. Suzimori develeoped a three chamber microactuator molded from silicone.
*Insert drawing of silicone muscle*</p>
</SubSection>

<SubSection title="Pneumatic Network (Pneunet)" id="Pneunet">
<p>In 2011, Dr. George Whitesides develeoped a silicone molded network of hollow chambers that could be actuated pneumatically.</p>

<Image src="/soro/pneunet-blue.svg">
<span class="fw-bold">Pneunet Actuator</span>: CAPTION.
</Image>

<p>From this time on, the field grew exponentially. Soft robot designs of varying size, material, and actuation type have been introduced for a wide array of biomimetic and bioinspired applications.</p>
</SubSection>

<SubSection title="Nature's Soft Actuators" id="Natures soft actuators">
<p>Throughout all of this time, scientists have been highlighting the ways in which nature has designed soft actuators, including pinecones.</p>

<Image src="/soro/PINECONES.jpg">
<span class="fw-bold">Natural Soft Actuators</span>: CAPTION.
</Image>
</SubSection>

